CAT = "cisaAcquisition"; SEG = "ACQ"
Q = [
("What is the main purpose of a feasibility study at the start of a system acquisition?",
 "To judge if a solution is justified", ["To write the detailed program specifications", "To train users on the new system", "To test the system's response times"],
 "A feasibility study weighs technical, economic and operational factors and the alternatives, such as build, buy or change nothing, so management can decide whether the project should go ahead."),
("An IS auditor is invited to join a development project team. How should the auditor take part without losing independence?",
 "Advise on controls, not design them", ["Approve the system for go-live", "Write the access control code", "Sign off the user acceptance results"],
 "The auditor can review and recommend, but designing or approving controls would mean later auditing their own work."),
("Midway through a project the expected benefits fall well below the cost to finish. What should happen?",
 "Revisit the business case before going on", ["Finish the project as it was first planned", "Cut testing to save the budget", "Hide the change from the sponsor"],
 "The business case should be checked at each stage gate. If it no longer holds, the sponsor and governance body decide whether to change course or stop."),
("Who is accountable for the business case and benefits of an IT project?",
 "The project sponsor", ["The lead software developer", "The IS auditor", "The test manager"],
 "The sponsor, usually a senior business manager, owns the business case, provides funding and is answerable for the project delivering its benefits."),
("Earned value analysis shows the earned value of completed work is lower than its actual cost. What does this mean?",
 "The project is over budget", ["The project is under budget", "The project is ahead of plan", "The scope has been reduced"],
 "A cost performance index below 1, earned value divided by actual cost, means the work done has cost more than planned for that work."),
("Users keep adding features to a project after requirements were agreed. Which control addresses this?",
 "Formal change control for scope", ["More developers on the team", "Longer working days for testers", "Skipping the design review"],
 "Scope changes should go through an approval process that weighs cost, schedule and risk, so the project does not drift without a decision."),
("A team uses agile sprints with little written documentation. What should an IS auditor check most closely?",
 "Controls are in the definition of done", ["Every sprint has its own written charter", "Developers fill in time sheets", "The team uses the waterfall model"],
 "Agile is fine if control requirements such as security and audit trails are part of what counts as complete. Otherwise they are dropped under time pressure."),
("What is a key risk of building a system by prototyping?",
 "Controls are left out of the final build", ["Users never see the system early", "Requirements become too formal", "The project always runs far behind schedule"],
 "Prototypes focus on what users see. If a prototype is promoted to production, controls and documentation that were never built may be missing."),
("Function point analysis is used to estimate what?",
 "The size of a software system", ["The speed of the network links", "The number of users", "The cost of hardware"],
 "Function points measure software size from the inputs, outputs, inquiries, files and interfaces it provides, which feeds effort and cost estimates."),
("Who should perform user acceptance testing of a new application?",
 "Business users in a test environment", ["Developers in production", "The auditor on live data", "The vendor alone at its own test site"],
 "User acceptance testing confirms the system meets business needs, so the people who will use it test it against agreed criteria, away from production."),
("Developers want to copy production customer data into the test environment. What should be required?",
 "Mask the personal data first", ["Copy it exactly as it is", "Give testers admin rights", "Delete the test logs after each run"],
 "Test environments are less protected, so personal data should be masked or replaced with synthetic data before it is used there."),
("Which changeover approach carries the lowest risk but the highest cost?",
 "Parallel changeover", ["Direct cutover", "Phased changeover", "Pilot site changeover"],
 "Running old and new systems side by side lets results be compared and the old system kept as a fallback, at the cost of double processing."),
("Which data conversion control best shows that all records moved to the new system intact?",
 "Reconciling counts and control totals", ["Checking the new screen layouts", "Asking users if the data looks right", "Timing how long conversion ran"],
 "Comparing record counts, hash totals and key balances between the old and new systems shows that nothing was lost, added or changed."),
("What must be in place before a major system goes live?",
 "A tested rollback plan", ["A new marketing campaign", "A larger help desk team", "A reduced backup schedule"],
 "If go-live fails, the organization must be able to return to the previous system quickly and safely. An untested fallback may not work when needed."),
("When should a post-implementation review normally be carried out?",
 "Once the system has settled in use", ["On the day it goes live", "Before user acceptance testing begins", "Only if the project failed"],
 "Waiting until the system settles lets the review judge whether benefits were achieved and requirements were met, rather than measuring launch problems."),
("A field accepts any value, but overtime hours per week should never exceed a set ceiling. Which input control fits?",
 "A limit check", ["A sequence check", "A completeness check", "A duplicate check"],
 "A limit check rejects or flags values above or below a set boundary. A sequence check looks for gaps or repeats in numbered items."),
("A payment system rejects a second invoice with the same vendor and invoice number. Which control is this?",
 "A duplicate check", ["A reasonableness check", "A range check", "A format check"],
 "A duplicate check compares new input with existing records to stop the same transaction being entered or paid twice."),
("Which processing control confirms that the output of one batch job matches the input of the next?",
 "Run-to-run totals", ["Field format edits", "Report distribution lists", "Screen prompts for users"],
 "Run-to-run totals carry control figures from one processing step to the next, showing that no data was lost or altered in between."),
("What is the main purpose of an output distribution log for printed payroll reports?",
 "To show who received each report", ["To check the printer's speed", "To store a copy of the report forever", "To change the report format"],
 "Logging output distribution supports confidentiality and accountability: it records that sensitive reports reached only authorized recipients."),
("Code is moved to production by a release team. What should the team confirm about each release?",
 "It was built from approved source code", ["It was written in a single language", "It was tested only by the developer who wrote it", "It was copied from a developer laptop"],
 "Release management builds from the controlled, approved version of the source, so the code in production is exactly what was reviewed and tested."),
("A company buys packaged software from a small vendor. Which arrangement protects it if the vendor goes out of business?",
 "A source code escrow agreement", ["A longer user license term", "A larger volume discount", "A vendor logo on the contract cover"],
 "Escrow lodges the source code with a third party, released to the customer if the vendor fails, so the software can still be maintained."),
("Who should formally accept the residual risk of a new system before it goes into production?",
 "The business owner of the system", ["The developer who built it", "The IS auditor who reviewed it", "The test lead who signed scripts"],
 "Accreditation is a management decision: the system owner accepts the remaining risk based on test and security results."),
("A team deploys through an automated CI/CD pipeline many times a day. Which control best keeps segregation of duties?",
 "Required peer approval in the pipeline", ["Monthly manual sign-off of all releases", "Fewer deployments each week", "Developer access to production"],
 "In continuous delivery, segregation is enforced by the pipeline itself, such as mandatory independent code review and approval gates before deployment."),
("An organization is building a machine learning model to approve loans. What should an IS auditor check about its training data?",
 "Its quality and potential bias", ["Its file names and folders", "The format of its output reports", "The speed of the training server"],
 "A model learns from its data. Incomplete or biased training data can produce unfair or wrong decisions, so data quality and bias testing need controls and evidence."),
]
